==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɡɹɪl/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-grill.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪl|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|grillen||to anger, provoke}}, from {{inh|en|ang|grillan}}, {{m|ang|griellan||to annoy, vex, offend}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*graljaną||to shout, make angry}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gʰer-||to rattle, make a noise, grumble}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|grulje||to make angry}}, {{cog|nl|grillen||to shudder, shiver}}, {{cog|nds|vergrellen||to anger, provoke}}, {{cog|de|grollen||to rumble}} and perhaps also with {{cog|fr|grouiller||to swarm}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|girl}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|Scotland|US}} To make [[angry]]; [[provoke]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|chiefly|Scotland}} To [[terrify]]; make [[tremble]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|chiefly|Scotland}} To [[tremble]]; [[shiver]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|Northern England|Scotland}} To [[snarl]]; [[snap]].

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|gril}}, {{m|enm|grille|t=harsh, rough, severe}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*griell}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*grellaz|t=angry}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gher-|t=to rattle, make a noise, grumble}}. Cognate with {{cog|de|grell||harsh, angry}}, {{cog|da|grel||shrill, glaring, dazzling}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|er|more}}

# [[harsh]], [[rough]], [[severe]]; [[cruel]]

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-|grills}}

# [[harm]]

===Etymology 3===
1655, from {{bor|en|fr|gril|notext=1}}, from {{der|en|frm}}, from {{der|en|fro|greïl}}, {{m|fro|graïl||gridiron}}, from {{m|fro|graïlle||grate, grating}}, from {{der|en|la|crātīcula||gridiron}}, diminutive of {{m|la|crātis||hurdle, wickerwork}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kor(ə)t-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*krāt-||to weave, twist, wattle; wicker}}. Related to {{m|en|griddle}}, {{m|en|hurdle}}.

====Alternative forms====
{{wikipedia|grille|grille}}

* {{l|en|grille}}

====Noun====
[[Image:Brazier.JPG|thumb|[[brazier|Brazier]] with '''grill''' and pot rest]]
{{en-noun}}

# A [[grating]]; a [[grid]] of [[wire]] or a sheet of material with a pattern of holes or slots, usually used to [[protect]] something while allowing the passage of [[air]] and [[liquid]]s. Typical uses: to allow air through a [[fan]] while preventing fingers or objects from passing; to allow people to talk to somebody, while preventing [[attack]].
#* {{RQ:Chmbrs YngrSt|I}}
#*: The house was a big elaborate limestone affair, evidently new. Winter sunshine sparkled on lace-hung casement, on glass marquise, and the burnished bronze foliations of '''grille''' and door.
# On a [[vehicle]], a [[slot]]ted cover as above, to protect and hide the [[radiator]], while admitting air to cool it.
# {{lb|en|UK}} A cooking device comprising a source of [[radiative]] [[heat]] and a means of holding food under it; a [[broiler]] in US English
# {{lb|en|US}} A cooking device comprising a source of radiative and [[convective]] heat and a means of holding food above it; a [[barbecue]].
#: {{ux|en|I put some peppers and mushrooms on the '''grill''' to go with dinner.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} A type of jewelry worn on the front teeth.
# {{lb|en|colloquial|by extension}} The front teeth regarded collectively.
# Food cooked on a grill.
#: {{ux|en|a packet of frozen cauliflower cheese '''grills'''}}
# A [[grillroom]]; a [[restaurant]] serving grilled food. <!--in Webster 1913-->
#: ''These coupons will get you a discount at Johnny's Bar and '''Grill'''.''
#* '''1986''', ''New York'' (volume 19, part 5, page 385)
#*: Everyone's meeting at the new '''grill''' in town! And everyone's having a real good time! They're drinking frozen blue Margaritas. Munching on Cajun popcorn shrimp. Laughing with old friends and getting to know new ones.
# {{lb|en|humorous}} {{misspelling of|girl|lang=en}} <!--needs moving to separate etymology section-->

